A site in several languages declares its locales, and marks each field that has a translation with `localized: true`. The translations of an entry stay in one entry, so they do not drift apart, and a relation names the entry once for all languages.

| Pattern | Use it for | Limits |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Localized fields | Pages and settings that exist in each language. | Each language has the same fields, and the languages publish together. |
| One collection per language | News or other content that is different in each language. | The editor does not connect a text with its translation. |

## Localized fields

Give the site its locales in `defineSite`, with the same names as Astro's `i18n`. Then add `localized: true` to each field that has a translation:

```ts
import { collection, defineSite, id, media, richText, text } from "@micacms/core";

export const site = defineSite({
  locales: ["de", "en", "fr"],
  defaultLocale: "de",
  // French falls back to English, then to the default locale.
  fallback: { fr: "en" },
  // As in Astro: the default locale has its prefix too, /de/…
  prefixDefaultLocale: true,
  collections: {
    pages: collection({
      label: "Pages",
      // The page in each language: the editor shows the one of the form's language.
      url: "/{locale}/{id}/",
      fields: {
        // The ID of the entry: the same for each language.
        id: id(),
        title: text({ label: "Title", required: true, localized: true }),
        body: richText({ label: "Text", localized: true }),
        // One image for all languages.
        image: media({ label: "Image", accept: "image" }),
        imageAlt: text({ label: "Alt text", localized: true }),
      },
    }),
  },
});
```

Mica stores a localized value in `index.json`, with one key for each locale, the default locale first, and rich text in one file for each locale:

```text
content/pages/about/index.json   { "title": { "de": "Über uns", "en": "About us", "fr": null } }
content/pages/about/body.de.mdoc
content/pages/about/body.en.mdoc
```

The rules:

- `required` and `default` apply to the default locale. A missing translation does not stop Save or Publish.
- Mica never writes a fallback value into a file. The files hold only the translations that editors wrote.
- An `object()`, a `repeater()` and the ID field of a collection cannot be localized. Localize the fields inside them.
- A localized `id()` cannot have `from`. Editors write the ID of each language.
- `localized: { fallback: false }` keeps a missing value empty on the website. Use it for text where the wrong language is worse than no text, such as legal text.
- The ID of an entry is unique, because it is the name of its folder. A localized `id()` among the fields of a collection, such as a page name for each language, must be unique in each locale, with its fallback: `npx mica check` and Publish report a value that another entry has.
- The title of an entry in the editor's lists, a list column, a sort and a new entry's ID use the default locale. When it is empty, they use the next locale that has a value.

### Add languages to a site

- A site that used an `object()` with one field for each language changes each object to one field with `localized: true`. When the keys of the object are the locales, the files do not change.
- A field that has values already changes their shape when it gets `localized`: `"title": "About"` must become `"title": { "de": "About" }`, and the rich-text file `body.mdoc` becomes `body.de.mdoc`. Move the values in the same commit as the model change, and run `npx mica check`. See [Change the model](/docs/model-changes/).
- Upgrade Mica together with `@micacms/astro`: an older Mica cannot read a model with locales.

### In the editor

The header of an entry or a singleton has a **Language** switcher, the default locale first. Each language tells how many of its texts are empty while the default locale has them, and marks a language with a problem. A localized field shows the input of the chosen language, and a language button at the end of its label. The button shows the field in the other languages, and it marks a missing translation. An empty text input shows its fallback in grey. A field that is the same in each language shows in each language. See [Edit content](/docs/editing/).

With `{locale}` in the `url` of a collection or a singleton, the preview, **Open on website** and the URL under the ID field show the page of the form's language. When the page in the live preview is in another language, the form changes to that language. Lists, relation pickers and the Publish page show the page of the default locale. `{locale}` is a part of the path alone, between two `/`. Without `prefixDefaultLocale: true`, the default locale's page has no prefix: `/{locale}/{id}/` is `/about/` in the default locale and `/en/about/` in English.

### The pages

Tell Astro the same languages in `astro.config.mjs`, with the same `prefixDefaultLocale`:

```js
export default defineConfig({
  i18n: { locales: ["de", "en", "fr"], defaultLocale: "de", routing: { prefixDefaultLocale: true } },
});
```

Make one page for each entry and language, for example in `src/pages/[lang]/[id].astro`. `localize` from `@micacms/astro` gives the data in one language. A missing value takes the value of the next locale in its chain: `fallback`, then `defaultLocale`.

```astro
---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { localize, renderRichText } from "@micacms/astro";
import { site } from "../../mica";

export async function getStaticPaths() {
  const pages = await getCollection("pages");
  return site.locales!.flatMap((lang) =>
    pages.map((page) => ({ params: { lang, id: page.id }, props: { data: localize(site, "pages", page.data, lang) } })),
  );
}

const { data } = Astro.props;
const { lang } = Astro.params;
// Editors write one address for all languages, such as /team/. The page adds its language.
const body = await renderRichText(data.body, data, { link: (href) => (href.startsWith("/") ? `/${lang}${href}` : href) });
---
<h1>{data.title}</h1>
<Fragment set:html={body} />
```

A missing translation shows the fallback here. Choose what your site does: show the fallback, or make no page.

- A link in rich text keeps the address that the editor wrote. The `link` option of `renderRichText` gives each link the address in the page's language.
- The labels of a `select()` field's options are the editor's. When the website shows the name of an option, keep that name in a localized field, for example in a singleton with the texts of the templates.
- An address with a page name for each language, such as `/{lang}/{slug}`, is your site's own. The `url` of the model names `{id}`, so the editor's links then go to a page by its ID.

## One collection per language

Give each language its own collection:

```ts
newsDe: collection({ label: "News (Deutsch)", fields: { title: text({ required: true }), id: id({ from: "title" }), body: richText() } }),
newsEn: collection({ label: "News (English)", fields: { title: text({ required: true }), id: id({ from: "title" }), body: richText() } }),
```

Each collection is a list of its own in the editor, with its own folder, URLs and order. When a text has a translation, a `relation()` field can name it.

## Limits

Each entry is in all languages of the site. A page without a translation shows the text of its fallback language. A list of the languages that an entry is in is an open request: [issue #365](https://github.com/micacms/mica/issues/365). The decision records [Localized fields](https://github.com/micacms/mica/blob/main/docs/adr/0042-localized-fields.md) and [The locale in a page's address](https://github.com/micacms/mica/blob/main/docs/adr/0043-locale-in-page-addresses.md) give the reasons for the design.
